.\" AXE modifications: describe the supported selectors and shared format path.
\fBps\fR prints a snapshot of the current processes. With no flags
it shows only the processes owned by the calling user and attached
to the current controlling terminal — the same minimal behaviour
that makes a bare \fBps\fR useful in a shell. Pass \fB\-e\fR or
\fB\-A\fR to widen the selection to every process on the system.
.PP
\fBps\fR has historically supported three flag conventions on
different Unixes — System V (POSIX, \fB\-e\fR), BSD (no leading
dash, \fBaux\fR), and GNU long forms. \fBprocutils\fR's \fBps\fR
implements a small but commonly-used subset of these:
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-e\fR / \fB\-A\fR / \fB\-\-all\fR — select every process.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-f\fR — full-format listing with columns UID, PID, PPID, C,
STIME, TTY, TIME, CMD. UID is the effective user name, TIME is
HH:MM:SS, and CMD is the full command line.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBa\fR selects all processes with a controlling terminal.
\fBx\fR selects all processes owned by your effective user, including
those without a terminal. Together, \fBax\fR selects every process.
\fBu\fR enables the user-oriented format; \fBaux\fR combines these.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBw\fR / \fBww\fR are accepted BSD width flags.
\fBr\fR restricts the complete selection to active processes.
\fBT\fR adds processes sharing the caller's controlling terminal.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-a\fR selects terminal processes except session leaders.
\fB\-x\fR selects your effective user's processes across terminals.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-p\fR \fIPID\fR\fB,\fR\fIPID\fR\fB,\fR\fI...\fR — restrict
output to specific PIDs.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-U\fR selects real user IDs. \fB\-u\fR / \fB\-\-user\fR selects
effective user IDs. User lists accept names or numeric IDs separated
by commas or spaces. An unknown user name is an error.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-L\fR / \fB\-T\fR — list threads with LWP / SPID columns.
BSD \fBH\fR lists threads in the BSD format.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-m\fR / BSD \fBm\fR — list each process summary followed by its threads.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-\-sort=KEY,\-KEY,...\fR / BSD \fBk\fR — sort by raw field values
using ascending or descending keys.
.PP
Custom output is available through \fB\-o\fR / \fB\-\-format\fR.
See FIELD DESCRIPTIONS for field names, aliases, and header overrides.
A selection with no processes prints any requested headers and exits
with status 1.
